2026-09-23T15:11:25.8424512Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8424888Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8425192Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8425352Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8425552Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8425715Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8425894Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8426063Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8426275Z         def inc(x):
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8426453Z             return x + 1
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8426640Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8426788Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8426952Z         def test_answer():
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8427147Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8427331Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8427481Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8427635Z     To execute it::
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8427802Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8427958Z         $ pytest
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8428183Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8428461Z         collected 1 items
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8428644Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8428797Z         test_sample.py F
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8428975Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8429170Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8429485Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8429760Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8429924Z             def test_answer():
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8430212Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8430428Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8430628Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8430833Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8431001Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8431265Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8431526Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8431782Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8432436Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8433121Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8433262Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8433420Z     Features
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8433584Z     --------
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8433736Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8434175Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8434748Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8434910Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8435249Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8435619Z       of test modules and functions
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8435829Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8436113Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8436518Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8436776Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8437043Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8437395Z       test suites out of the box
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8437588Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8437750Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8437940Z     
2026-09-23T15:11:25.8438380Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
